   "summary": "Measured self-extinguishment limits (MOC, % O2 by volume) of 18 spacecraft materials in the NASA-STD-6001 upward flammability test, at total pressures from 7 to 17.3 psia (48-119 kPa). For 17 of 22 materials the MOC barely changes with pressure between 48 and 119 kPa; halogenated materials (PTFE, Kel-F, Zotek, Armalon) and Nomex become harder to burn at low pressure. Flammability depends more on oxygen concentration than on oxygen partial pressure.",
   "details": "Values are the MOC (highest O2 concentration at which all samples self-extinguish) read from Table 2 of the source. Below about 41 kPa (6 psia) MOCs rise steeply. Black Fox interpolates these points in log-pressure for its fire-screening model.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "title": "Oxygen Partial Pressure and Oxygen Concentration Flammability: Can They Be Correlated? (NASA WSTF, Journal of ASTM International)",
     "url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20160001047",
     "ntrs_id": "20160001047"
    }
   ],
