  {
   "id": "zero-g-flammability-margin",
   "title": "Materials burn at lower oxygen in 0 g than in the 1 g Test 1 (up to 4 % O2)",
   "kind": "finding",
   "year": 2008,
   "gravity": [
    "zero_g"
   ],
   "topic": "combustion_fire",
   "org": "NASA Glenn Research Center",
   "keywords": [
    "0g MOC",
    "ULOI",
    "margin of safety",
    "Ultem",
    "Nomex",
    "Mylar",
    "low-speed flow",
    "drop tower",
    "concurrent flow",
    "opposed flow",
    "flame spread correlation"
   ],
   "summary": "Drop-tower tests in a low-speed flow tunnel (0-30 cm/s) at exploration pressures showed that Ultem 1000, Nomex HT90-40 and Mylar G burn in 0 g at oxygen concentrations up to 4 % lower than their 1 g NASA-STD-6001 limits. Ultem: 1 g mean limit 25.25 %, 0 g mean limit 22.5 % (difference -2.75 %), inside the ISS 21 +/- 2 % band.",
   "details": "Opposed-flow spread over thin fuels correlates with X_O2^0.9 * P^0.05 (Magee and McAlevy); along the normoxic curve opposed spread over Kimwipes rises from about 1 to 5 cm/s as O2 goes from 21 to 80 %. Concurrent spread rises roughly linearly with flow velocity and oxygen and with the square root of pressure (linear fit y = 0.2498 x + 0.4038, R^2 = 0.97); at 30 cm/s it rises only from about 3 to 4 cm/s along the normoxic curve. Over the nominal spacecraft ventilation range of 5-20 cm/s opposed spread is faster.",
   "sources": [
    {
